'''Vireonidæ''' (vir-ē̤-on′i-dē), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < ''Vireo(n-)'' + ''-idæ''.] A family of small dentirostral

readings.c1s2:
oscine passerine birds, related to the ''Laniidæ'' or shrikes; the vireos or greenlets. {{smaller|They have a hooked bill, rictal bristles, ten primaries, scutellate tarsi, and toes coherent at the base. They are all small birds, under 7 inches long, of simple and mostly greenish coloration, and are confined to America, where they are migratory in the northern parts. The genera are ''Vireo'', specially characteristic of North America, containing some 30 species in its several sections, with ''Laletes, Cyclarhis, Hylophilus, Vireolanius'', and ''Neochloe'', and probably ''Dulus'' and ''Phœnicomanes. N. brevipennis'' is a Mexican type; ''L. osburni'' is peculiar to Jamaica. The ''Vireonidæ'' are remarkable in possessing either ten, or apparently only nine, primaries in closely related forms, owing to the variable development of the spurious first primary, which is sometimes quite rudimentary. The species of ''Vireo'' are insectivorous, and inhabit woodland and shrubbery, have an earnest and voluble, often highly melodious song, weave pensile nests, and lay spotted eggs. See the phrase-names under ''Vireo'', and cuts under ''Dulus, Hylophilus, redeye, solitary, Vireo'', and ''whip-tom-kelly''.}}

'''Vireoninæ''' (vir″ē̤-ō-nī′nē), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < ''Vireo''(''n''-) + ''-inæ''.] The ''Vireonidæ'' rated as a subfamily of ''Laniidæ''.

'''vireonine''' (vir′ē̤-ō-nin), ''a.'' Of or pertaining to the ''Vireonidæ''; resembling or related to a vireo.
{{smaller block|The usual ''Vireonine'' style of architecture . . . a closely-matted cup swung pensile from a forked twig, nearly hemispherical in contour, and rather large for the size of the bird. {{right|''Coues'', Birds of Colorado Valley, I. 523.}}}}

'''Vireosylvia''' (vir″ē̤-ō-sil′vi-ä), ''n.'' [NL. (Bonaparte, 1838), < ''Vireo'' + ''Sylvia'', q. v.] A genus of vireos, or section of ''Vireo'', including the larger greenlets with comparatively slender bill, as the common red-eyed vireo, the black-whiskered vireo, the whip-tom-kelly, and others. See cut under ''greenlet''.

